\section{An Instruction's Width Can Affect Its Own Range}

John R. Levine's simple storage-allocation example places each module after the lengths and alignment padding of preceding modules \cite[chapter~4]{levine2000linkers}. This is a linker example. The same dependence exists within an assembler's section, but here fragment lengths can themselves depend on the offsets computed from the layout. Chapter~2 shows why the dependence is target-specific: the range of a relative branch follows from the architecture's displacement format. In x86-64, Intel's \texttt{JMP} table provides both a two-byte short jump (\texttt{EB} plus a signed byte) and a five-byte near jump (\texttt{E9} plus a signed four-byte field). Both use the address of the next instruction as the base.

An assembler may start with a short encoding for each eligible local jump. If one fails its range test, widening it moves later positions; a second jump may now fail even though it passed in the previous layout. Choosing a form and then filling the displacement without reconsidering the other instructions is not sufficient. Here \emph{relaxation} means changing encoding choices while laying out an input section. Linkers can perform their own relaxation with different information and ABI restrictions, but the local jumps below are settled before the linker sees an object.

