\section{Make One Widening Force Another}

Save this non-executed layout fixture as \texttt{cascade.s} and assemble it with \verb|as --64 -o cascade.o cascade.s|.

\begin{verbatim}
.text
    # A short first jump fits if the second is also short.
    jmp .Lfirst
    jmp .Lsecond
    .fill 125, 1, 0x90
.Lfirst:
    # These bytes push only the second jump past its limit.
    .fill 3, 1, 0x90
.Lsecond:
    ret
\end{verbatim}

In an all-short trial, the next-instruction offsets are two and four. The targets are $4+125=129$ and $129+3=132$, giving displacements $127$ and $128$. Only the second overflows. Widening it adds three bytes between the first jump and its target, so the first displacement becomes $130$ and it too must widen. The settled target offsets are $135$ and $138$. The jump bases are now five and ten, giving $135-5=130$ and $138-10=128$. The second displacement is unchanged by its own widening in this fixture: both its base and its forward target moved by the same amount.

\Cref{fig:visual-283} tracks which short form fails after each change of layout.

