Use \verb|objdump -dr cascade.o|. The first ten bytes are \texttt{E9 82 00 00 00 E9 80 00 00 00}. They are evidence of the settled layout, not of any particular internal sequence of passes in GNU \texttt{as}. The assembler does not emit a relocation for either local displacement here. As a boundary test, reduce the second fill to two bytes. Now both displacements are $127$ in the all-short layout and neither needs to grow: expect \texttt{EB 7F EB 7F} at the start. That one-byte source adjustment saves six more bytes of encoding in this fixture.

\section{Prove Termination Separately from Validity}

In a simple implementation, give each relaxable instruction a finite list of forms from shortest to longest. For a given vector of form choices, compute a complete layout: fragment offsets, padding, and label values. Check the current displacements against their chosen forms, promote failing sites that have wider forms available, and lay out again. Compute the checks from a consistent snapshot rather than testing some branches with old offsets and others with new ones. A round that changes a choice strictly increases the sum of form ranks. It is bounded above by the sum of maximum ranks, so there cannot be infinitely many such rounds.

Termination does not prove a legal encoding exists. When no more choices change, recompute and validate every range and field under that exact layout. Diagnose any remaining overflow. A section-relative label still moves between trials, so any fixup cached as an absolute section offset must be recomputed or derived anew from a fragment and an intra-fragment position. Emission must agree with the validated layout, including the padding and any symbol or metadata offsets. If the widest form has an unresolved target outside the section, the local displacement proof does not apply; use a form and relocation permitted by the ABI or report that it cannot be represented.

An independent check of the settled fixture starts by decoding the actual lengths and signed fields, not reading the assembler's former choices. Adding the decoded displacements to the two bases recovers offsets $135$ and $138$. Checking only that the output starts with two near-jump opcodes would miss a stale patch left over from the round with just one near jump.

\section{Alignment Complicates Size Optimization}

Suppose a two-byte branch at offset eleven ends at thirteen, followed by unrestricted sixteen-byte alignment and then its target. There are three bytes of padding and the displacement is three. Widen a different instruction earlier in the section by three bytes. The same short branch now starts at fourteen and ends at sixteen. The alignment adds nothing, so the target stays at sixteen and the displacement drops to zero. Consequently the rank argument does not prove that every displacement grows or that every promotion remains necessary. A growth-only algorithm trades possible extra bytes for a finite convergence policy. An optimizer that tries shortening again needs its own policy to avoid cycles and must validate the entire affected layout.
